@dutchiris 

I think the phrase is actually a contronym! Meaning that its definition is also its antonym. "Hot minute" has been observed to mean "in an instant" in some contexts and "in a while" in others. For example, "I haven't seen you in a hot minute" means that it's been a while, and "I'll get back to you in a hot minute" means that they'll get back to you as soon as possible.

From what I could find, it first meant instantly, but people kept using it sarcastically to mean a long period of time that it became cemented as an additional definition despite the paradox.

